WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.116
Environment values should be validated
according to what they mean.

2
00:00:04.196 --> 00:00:08.248
The program should not treat all variables
as generic strings after startup.

3
00:00:08.328 --> 00:00:10.946
A boolean, number, path, list, mode,
identifier,

4
00:00:11.026 --> 00:00:15.203
or feature selector each has different
validation needs and different failure

5
00:00:15.283 --> 00:00:15.844
behavior.

6
00:00:15.924 --> 00:00:19.042
Boolean and mode variables should allow
only known values.

7
00:00:19.122 --> 00:00:23.298
If a setting supports enabled and
disabled, or a small set of operational modes,

8
00:00:23.378 --> 00:00:27.431
the parser should accept that known
vocabulary and reject ambiguous values.

9
00:00:27.511 --> 00:00:32.124
Silent interpretation of unexpected
strings can produce surprising behavior and make

10
00:00:32.204 --> 00:00:34.448
operational mistakes harder to diagnose.

11
00:00:34.528 --> 00:00:36.710
Numeric values need careful conversion.

12
00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:39.596
The code should check whether conversion
succeeded,

13
00:00:39.676 --> 00:00:43.977
whether extra characters were present,
whether the value fits the intended type,

14
00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:46.489
and whether it falls within the allowed
range.

15
00:00:46.569 --> 00:00:50.372
Values that drive allocation, timeouts,
retry counts, logging limits,

16
00:00:50.452 --> 00:00:53.693
or concurrency should have practical
minimums and maximums.

17
00:00:53.773 --> 00:00:56.828
Path values deserve the same care as other
external paths.

18
00:00:56.908 --> 00:00:59.464
They should have length limits, expected
forms,

19
00:00:59.544 --> 00:01:03.285
approved roots where appropriate, and
defined behavior when invalid.

20
00:01:03.365 --> 00:01:07.604
A path from the environment is still
external input unless deployment provides a

21
00:01:07.684 --> 00:01:10.365
stronger trust boundary and the code
documents it.

22
00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:13.873
List-style variables need clear separator
rules, entry limits,

23
00:01:13.953 --> 00:01:17.756
empty-entry handling, duplicate handling,
and per-entry validation.

24
00:01:17.836 --> 00:01:22.013
Without those rules, a list can become an
open-ended parsing problem or a way for

25
00:01:22.093 --> 00:01:24.961
accidental configuration to change search
behavior.

26
00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:29.156
Missing or malformed variables should lead
to safe defaults or clear failure,

27
00:01:29.236 --> 00:01:30.420
not guessed behavior.
